WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:32.167
Define success. For this example, success means a correct booking response within its latency objective. A homepage response alone does not measure the same user outcome.

2
00:00:32.167 --> 00:01:11.583
Spread the copies. Applications run in separate zones behind a resilient entry point. The goal is to avoid losing every application copy to the same local failure.

3
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:53.917
Lose one zone. Zone A becomes unavailable. Detection and rerouting consume time, and requests already in flight may fail. The objective allows an explicitly measured error budget.

4
00:01:53.917 --> 00:02:34.833
Use remaining capacity. Zone B receives new work. It must have enough capacity, network access and working credentials to serve the actual user operation.

5
00:02:34.833 --> 00:03:08.417
Follow the data. Both application copies need a suitable data service. A single unprotected database can still interrupt bookings even when both application zones are healthy.

6
00:03:08.417 --> 00:03:57.167
Test the objective. Run a controlled failure and measure booking success and recovery time. Availability is demonstrated by user outcomes, not by counting boxes in a diagram.
